From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mcla-x86 SDK patch layer
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 04a0: swap_direct_present: the swap shows the front buffer's host render target without loading it, and the gamma ramp goes into the presenter's paint

Source: Ours, written for this SDK (O2; R2 lever 4 and S2 lever 8: $WORK/scratch/R2/REPORT.md, $WORK/scratch/S2/REPORT.md). The
  SDK's swap, direct resolve, shared memory watch and presenter code are used as they are. The pixel shader is a port of the
  SDK's apply_gamma_table (the same table lookup) with the paint's pixel index; compiled with fxc from the HLSL in this patch
Licence: GPL-3 (owner's own work, part of mcla-x86); the patched files are ReXGlue SDK code (BSD-3-Clause)
Purpose: Cut GPU time in the D3D12 swap at 1080p (R2: about 0.2-0.35 ms on the 880M). A swap was: the game's front buffer resolve
  (render target -> shared memory) -> texture load (shared memory -> texture) -> apply-gamma compute (texture -> guest output image)
  -> presenter paint (guest output image -> back buffer): 2 full-screen passes more than the picture needs. Cvar
  swap_direct_present (default FALSE, hot reload): when the front buffer was resolved, whole, from ONE host 8_8_8_8 render target
  by a direct resolve and nothing has written the memory or the render target since, the swap reads that render target instead
  of the loaded texture (no texture load: the apply-gamma pass reads the render target). Cvar swap_direct_present_fused (default
  true, with swap_direct_present): and when the guest output thread paints the frame right after the refresh as a plain 1:1 copy
  of an 8-bit image (no scaling, no effects, SDR, no frame generation, no UI overlay painting), the apply-gamma pass is dropped:
  the presenter's paint maps the ramp over the render target while it copies it to the back buffer - one pass. Cvar
  swap_direct_present_check (default 0): every Nth swap that could use it, the picture is made the old way as well, both the
  new ways are drawn next to it, read back and compared, and the largest difference per channel is logged
  ("swap_direct_present_check: ... 0/0/0/0" is exact). The resolve still writes the front buffer to shared memory. Not built or run
  (every changed file passes clang++ -fsyntax-only with the SDK's Release flags, except the PCH; see Checks).
Bucket: REDO

04a0-swap-direct-present.patch (mcla-x86 O2)

Depends on 0490 (it patches the same Resolve and texture cache header lines). Series entry, after 0490-dual-write-resolve:
  name = "04a0-swap-direct-present", source = "local", path = "patches/sdk/04a0-swap-direct-present.patch", subdir = "", bucket = "REDO".

What reads the front buffer's guest memory (the question: is the memory write still needed?). Found: nothing in this SDK or in this
repo's host code. The swap itself (the texture load, which this removes for the swap), the CPU readbacks (d3d12_readback_resolve,
readback_resolve rules: none for the front buffer, only the vinyl editor's 512x512), and Presenter::CaptureGuestOutput, which has
no caller in the SDK or in mcla-x86 and reads the guest output IMAGE, not the guest memory (tools/run_game.py takes screenshots of
the window with CopyFromScreen, which sees the presented picture). The game's own CPU code could read the memory: unknown, so the
resolve is NOT skipped (the 16.8 MB texture load and a pass are what is saved). A front buffer resolve that is skipped altogether
when nothing reads it would save a third pass; it needs an owner decision.

Design.
  * Recording (d3d12/render_target_cache.cpp NoteSwapDirectResolve, called for every resolve that copied): a resolve qualifies when it
    was direct, read one single-sampled k_8_8_8_8 colour render target at the resolve's own base (no offset in the render target
    or the EDRAM, at the texture's origin), at 1x, not stretched by the FSR upscaling (TaaSqueezingScene), to a k_8_8_8_8
    destination with no exponent bias, not an array slice. A red/blue swap in the resolve (RB_COPY_DEST_INFO copy_dest_swap,
    which Direct3D 9 sets for ARGB front buffers) is allowed: the swap's SRV component mapping gets the same swap composed
    into it ({2, 1, 0, 3} on the channels the fetch constant's swizzle reads), so it is exact. Then the destination texel (x, y) is the render
    target's texel (x, y) and the bytes are the same up to the endian swap that the swap's fetch constant undoes (compared at
    the swap: base address, pitch, endianness; the texture may not be larger than the resolved area). One record per destination
    address (4, the least recent replaced), so another small 8_8_8_8 resolve does not evict the front buffer's.
    The recorded memory range must be GPU-written valid data when it is recorded (SharedMemory::AreAllPagesValidAndGpuWritten,
    asked under the global critical region that a write to the memory also takes).
  * Staleness: (1) the shared memory's global watch (any CPU write, any other GPU write, a ClearCache) bumps a per-record counter
    when it overlaps the recorded range - the next resolve of the same memory included; (2) a D3D12RenderTarget counts the times
    it is put in a state it can be written in (render target, depth write, UAV, copy / resolve destination: every draw, clear,
    ownership transfer and HDR copy does, SetResourceState), and has a serial that a trimmed and made-again render target does not
    keep (the record keeps keys, FindRenderTargetByKey, no pointers). A resolve that clears the render target after copying
    bumps it, so it never counts. The direct resolve leaves the render target in the non-pixel read state, so every later write
    must go through SetResourceState.
  * The swap (D3D12CommandProcessor::IssueSwap): PrepareSwapDirect reads the fetch constant 0 (D3D12TextureCache::GetSwapTextureInfo:
    2D, k_8_8_8_8, tiled, one level, not scaled; the SRV swizzle of RequestSwapTexture is kept) and asks the cache. If it matches,
    the render target's SRV stands in for the swap texture (its size is the fetch constant's: the picture is the top-left corner)
    and the texture isn't loaded. HDR output and frame generation read the swap texture too: they get it loaded (inside the
    refresher, before it asks for descriptors). The render target is moved to the read state and recorded as that.
  * Fused paint: D3D12Presenter::FusedGammaState per guest output (mailbox) image. The refresher asks fused_gamma_possible()
    (IsFusedGammaPaintPossible: the guest output thread owns painting and paints right after - Presenter::
    LockForGuestOutputThreadPaintCheck, which also excludes UI overlay painting - the connection is SDR and paintable, no
    frame generation, and GetGuestOutputPaintFlow for the image says one bilinear pass whose output is the image's size) and, if
    so, gives the render target and the ramp (SRVs) instead of writing the image; both go to the read state for pixel shaders. The
    paint (guest_output_gamma_table_ps, a root signature of its own with the same three parameter indices, a pipeline for the
    swap chain format) draws the same rectangle with the output's offset: for a pixel the texel under it, its ramp value, which
    is what the apply-gamma pass wrote and the 1:1 bilinear paint of that image would hand on. The paint makes a descriptor pair
    in its own heap per command allocator and keeps references to the resources for the GPU until the allocator is reused.
    Only a paint in the guest output thread right after the refresh may use the image (the source is a live render target);
    any other paint of such an image (the mode changed in between, a window repaint) shows the older frame still in its texture
    (a texture is offered for this only after it was written once), and CaptureGuestOutput of one says no.
  * Fused paint exactness: the same table lookup on the same 8-bit value, the same float from the ramp as the UNORM10 texture
    sample would give (a 10-bit value over 1023, exact), the same alpha 1, to the same B8G8R8A8 render target. The only thing
    the old paint added was the bilinear filter at texel centres, which only hands the texel on (the weights are 0 and 1 on any
    GPU that rounds the fraction to 8 bits). Not proven without a run: swap_direct_present_check says it.

Diagnostics (added after V3b-dwsw showed 0 of ~600 swaps/10 s qualifying with no "can be shown" line, so no resolve ever
passed the rules): every picture-sized (640x360 or more) colour resolve that doesn't qualify is noted with a reason, and the
swap's "why not" names the reason for the swap texture's address instead of a general one ("front buffer resolve not direct ...",
"... render target isn't a single-sampled k_8_8_8_8 one", "... starts inside the render target (a source offset)", "... EDRAM
base isn't the surface's", "... destination isn't a k_8_8_8_8 non-array texture", "... exponent bias", "... destination offset",
"... memory isn't valid GPU-written data when recorded", "... squeezed (FSR upscaled) scene", "... resolution scale above 1").
Each NEW reason for a destination is logged once (at most 12) with the resolve's fields:
  swap_direct_present: the resolve to 0x08F28000 (1920x1080, destination pitch 1920, endian 2, format 6, swap red/blue 1, exp bias 0,
  array 0, destination offset 0,0) doesn't qualify: <reason>; EDRAM base B (the surface's S), area offset 0,0, source <render target
  name> (colour format N, 1x MSAA, depth 0), source offset 0x00000000
and the first 3 swaps without a record log the swap texture next to the records and rejects:
  swap_direct_present: the swap's texture 0x08F28000 1920x1080 pitch 1920 endian 2 has no record (<reason>): record ...; rejected ...;
Read those two lines in the next run to see which condition fails.

Checks to run (VER), in this order, each on a frozen frame and on the moving bench:
  1. swap_direct_present=true swap_direct_present_fused=false swap_direct_present_check=60: the log has
     "swap_direct_present: a resolve of the host render target ... can be shown without loading it" once, a line every 10 s
     "swap_direct_present: N swaps in 10.0 s: N made from the render target (0 with the gamma ramp applied in the presenter's paint),
     0 from the loaded texture; K checks", and "swap_direct_present_check: apply-gamma image #n ... 0/0/0/0, 0 pixels differ".
     A non-zero difference or a reason list ("the render target was written since the resolve" ...) is the finding.
  2. the same with swap_direct_present_fused=true: also "swap_direct_present_check: fused paint #n ... 0/0/0/0".
  3. swap_direct_present=true, check off: GPU time of the swap (PresentMon MsGPUBusy, or P1's timestamps), and the picture
     against the old path on a screenshot of a still scene (identical).
  The fused paint needs host_present_from_non_ui_thread (guest output thread presenting: no implicit vsync, no UI drawers, no
  present_ui_overlay, no frame generation) - otherwise the log says "(0 with the gamma ramp applied in the presenter's paint)".

Interplay with 0490 (resolve_dual_write): with the swap not loading the front buffer, no texture is ever made for it, so the dual
write finds none (fallback "no texture") and writes nothing extra; a check swap loads the texture (once in N), after which
the dual write for it starts and its "dual writes unused" counter counts the front buffer, which is cosmetic.

Not done / limits: only the table gamma path (k_8_8_8_8 front buffer); k_2_10_10_10 (PWL ramp), FXAA (the pass still reads the
render target, but isn't fused), HDR, frame generation, FSR/CAS effects and scaled output use the old paint; resolution_scale > 1 and
the squeezed FSR scene (TaaSqueezingScene) are never recorded; a front buffer in several tile resolves (one record per tile
address, the swap's texture must match one) falls back. The front buffer resolve is not skipped (see above).

Changed files: include/rex/graphics/{shared_memory.h, pipeline/render_target/cache.h, d3d12/render_target_cache.h,
d3d12/texture_cache.h, d3d12/command_processor.h}, include/rex/ui/{presenter.h, d3d12/d3d12_presenter.h},
src/graphics/{shared_memory.cpp, d3d12/render_target_cache.cpp, d3d12/texture_cache.cpp, d3d12/command_processor.cpp},
src/ui/{presenter.cpp, d3d12/d3d12_presenter.cpp}, and new src/ui/shaders/guest_output_gamma_table.ps.hlsl and
src/ui/shaders/bytecode/d3d12_5_1/guest_output_gamma_table_ps.h (fxc 10.0.26100.0, ps_5_1 /O3 /Qstrip_reflect /Qstrip_debug
/Qstrip_priv, 632 bytes, the command is in the HLSL file).

Checks done: clang++ 20.1.8 -fsyntax-only (Release flags and defines of the SDK build, no PCH, -w) of the 6 changed .cpp files and
of d3d12_presenter_frame_generation.cpp, d3d12/{frame_generation,temporal_aa,pipeline_cache,graphics_system}.cpp,
ui/vulkan/vulkan_presenter.cpp: 0 errors. git apply --check --cached of this diff on series 88 + 0490: clean. Not built, not run.

